Subject: RegExp census: per-regexp and per-builtin counters behind QJS_RE_STATS

What this does
  - quickjs.c: a statistics block before `js_string_match` and hooks in the RegExp and String builtins. With `QJS_RE_STATS=1` (report on stderr at exit) or `QJS_RE_STATS=<path>` the process counts, per regular expression (source + flags) and per builtin: calls, cycles, matcher attempts, hits, average match length, subject length, distance scanned before a hit or to the end on a miss, matcher strategy, capture groups, regexp objects created and `lre_compile` calls.
  - `js_regexp_exec`, `test`, `[Symbol.match]`, `[Symbol.matchAll]`, `[Symbol.replace]`, `[Symbol.search]`, `[Symbol.split]` and `String.prototype.match/matchAll/search/replace/replaceAll/split` keep their names and addresses (the pristine-RegExp checks compare the function pointer) and become a wrapper over a `_body` function. `js_regexp_match` becomes a wrapper over `js_regexp_match_raw`.

Why we need it
  The regexp project orders its work by where the time goes per literal and per builtin; the interpreter profile only says "backtracking matcher 51%".

Soundness
  The variable is read once in a constructor function. With it unset every hook is one test of `re_st_on` and the behaviour is the original.

Failure modes
  Builtin cycle columns are inclusive (a replace that runs exec counts the exec time in both rows); the top-level total counts depth-0 calls only. A pattern with characters above 127 is keyed by UTF-8 bytes where it is compiled and by code units where the object is created, so those two counts land in different rows. More than 4096 distinct patterns share one overflow row.

